<<<
**amusive** is best avoi[ded]; [?] in fact [?] no [?] obstacle better served by *amusing*; [?] spite of its actual age it suggests either ignorance or [?]TY-HUNTING.
===
**amusive** is best avoided. It serves in fact (though differentiation might have taken place) no purpose not better served by *amusing*; & in spite of its actual age it always suggests either ignorance or ^^NOVELTY-HUNTING^^.
>>>

<<<
**anachronic, anachronistic.** The long form is better (1) because, as to the place of its accent (ăn'ăkronĭk, ană'kronĭ'stĭk; cf. ană'k[?]), its connexion with its noun is instantly apparent, & (2) bec[ause it] is therefore commoner.
===
**anachronic, anachronistic.** The long form is better (1) because owing to the places of its accents (ăn*a*krŏ'nĭk, *a*nă'kronĭ'stĭk; cf. *a*nă'kronĭzm) its connexion with its noun is more instantly apparent, & (2) because it is therefore commoner.
>>>
